<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  VM live test: upload to a RustFS (S3) bucket, whole object in one PUT.

  Byte for byte the dialog of vm-minio.xml, pointed at a different S3 server, and that is the point:
  two independent implementations accepting the same fixture is what shows the shape is S3 rather than
  one vendor's reading of it. MinIO covers the deployments already in the field; RustFS covers a
  maintained server, MinIO having archived its open-source one. Everything vm-minio.xml says about the
  limits applies here unchanged, and was re-measured against RustFS 1.0.0:

  - NO CREDENTIAL, on purpose. Authenticated S3 requires an AWS SigV4 signature, an HMAC recomputed
    over every request. REST does only header-based HTTP auth (Basic, NTLM, Kerberos, Negotiate) and
    has neither a clock nor an HMAC among its placeholders, so no configuration can produce one. The
    bucket vm/setup_rustfs.sh creates grants anonymous s3:PutObject on the orc-upload/ prefix and
    nothing else: anonymous GET and LIST both answer 403 AccessDenied. Anyone holding this file can
    write there - which is why the bucket is versioned, so an overwrite adds a version instead of
    destroying a collection, and why a real deployment of this shape must seal its output (CMS
    encryption) rather than trust the bucket.
  - NO CHUNKING. S3 splits a large object with multipart upload, which needs the UploadId out of a
    POST ?uploads XML response BODY - this engine captures response headers only - and a final request
    whose body is XML built from every part's ETag. Neither is expressible here, so there is no
    rustfs-chunked companion to this fixture and an object is capped at S3's 5 GiB single-PUT limit.
  - NO RESUME. A PUT replaces an object rather than appending, so the HEAD -> offset pattern of
    rest-resume.xml would upload the tail as the whole new object.

  RustFS serves the sink's own server.crt on 8452, so the committed pin applies unchanged and nothing
  here needs insecure="true": the transport is verified even though the destination is anonymous.
-->
<dfir-orc>
    <upload method="REST" server="%ORC_UPLOAD_SERVER%"
            https_security="tls1.3, tls1.2" operation="copy"
            server_cert_pin="">
        <rest http_method="PUT" path="/orc/orc-upload/{upload.filename}" body="{upload.local_path}"/>
    </upload>
</dfir-orc>
